/Alt (Figure 5: Task objective divergence is measured as the Kullback-Leibler Divergence \(KLD\) between the robot's estimated task belief distribution and the correct motion goal. Results show that high-competency robots with predictable or legible motions exhibit lower task objective divergence compared to low-competency robots with predictable or legible motions. Descriptive statistics are included in Sec. V-A.␀)
/K [0 790 0 R]
>>
endobj
271 0 obj
<<
/P 13 0 R
/A 1433 0 R
/C /adbe_style_006000
/S /Figure
/Pg 1183 0 R
/ID (AD241024-0845-1125-ADBE-000000011579)
/Alt (Figure 6: This figure provides the timing of the first correction during the robot's trajectory. High-competency robots with legible or predictable motions are corrected earlier than low-competency robots with legible or predictable motions. Timing is reported in seconds. Descriptive statistics are included in Sec. V-A.␀)
/K [1 791 0 R]
>>
endobj
272 0 obj
<<
/P 13 0 R
/A 1434 0 R
/C /adbe_style_006001
/S /Figure
/Pg 1183 0 R
/ID (AD241024-0845-1125-ADBE-000000011581)
/Alt (Figure 7: The proportion of the robot's trajectory left untraveled at the time of correction. High-competency robots with legible or predictable motion styles exhibit a larger proportion of untraveled trajectories when corrected, compared to legible and predictable motions in low competency levels. Descriptive statistics are included in Sec. V-A.␀)
/K [2 792 0 R]
>>
endobj
273 0 obj
<<
/P 13 0 R
